lisptc — semantic: the % forms, judged by meaning instead of by value.
Needs OPENROUTER_API_KEY in the top-level .env. Send one form at a time
and the REPL prints its value.

A % in front of a plain form asks the same question by meaning.

Probabilities: a yes/no question answered with a number from 0 to 1,
so you set the threshold.
(%p "I was charged twice, please give my money back" "is a refund request")
(%p "the app crashes when I open settings" "is a refund request")

%p-each asks every item in one call.
(%p-each '("love it" "worst purchase ever" "it is fine") "is a complaint")

Picking: %choose returns the option exactly as written. :none lets it
refuse every option, and :min sets how sure it must be.
(%choose "the app crashes when I open settings" '(:billing :technical :other))
(%choose "what a lovely sunset" '(:billing :technical) :none :unsure :min 0.6)

Rating: %score is 0 (very low) to 4 (very high), or your own levels.
(%score "the server is down and customers cannot pay" "urgency")
(%score "can you change my avatar color" "urgency" '("can wait" "today" "right now"))

Predicates give t or nil. Equal values and numbers answer without a call.
(%= "Hi there!" "Hello!")
(%= 2 2.0)

:context compares by one aspect only.
(%= "Get out." "Please leave." :context "politeness")
(%< "a cat" "a horse" :by "size")
(%is-a '(:name "Ada" :born 1815) "person data")

%enough? returning nil means ask the user rather than guess.
(%enough? '("from Paris" "to Tokyo") "to book the flight")

Lists: the whole list goes in one call.
(setq animals '("granite" "golden retriever" "oak tree" "tabby cat"))
(%find "a pet" animals)
(%filter "a pet" animals)
(%count-if "alive" animals)

(setq tickets
  '("typo on the pricing page"
    "checkout fails for every customer"
    "dark mode would be nice"))
(%sort tickets :by "urgency" :desc t)
(%max tickets :by "impact on revenue")

Branching: one call picks the clause, and its body runs as plain Lisp.
(defun route (ticket)
  (%cond ticket
    ("asks for a refund" :refund)
    ("reports a bug" :bug)
    (t :escalate)))
(route "you billed me for a plan I cancelled, I want it back")
(route "the export button does nothing")
(%case "38 degrees and no cloud" (:hot "stay in") (:mild "walk") (t "check again"))

Embeddings: meaning as numbers. %similarity is a cosine, near 1 for
the same meaning.
(length (%embed "a refund request"))
(%similarity "I want my money back" "please refund me")
(%similarity "I want my money back" "the weather is nice")

The first of two near-duplicates is kept.
(%remove-duplicates '("reset my password" "I forgot my password" "cancel my plan"))
(%cluster '("refund please" "charged twice" "app crashes" "login loops forever"))
(head (%sort tickets :near "payments are broken") 1)
(%outliers '("apple" "banana" "cherry" "carburetor"))

With :examples, %choose picks the option whose example is nearest,
with no probability call.
(%choose "my card was declined" '(:billing :technical)
    :examples '((:billing "I was charged twice") (:technical "the app crashes")))

Fallback: a % call can time out or fail, so guard the costly guesses.
(try
    (%p "ship it?" "is safe to deploy" :timeout 1)
    (catch (e)
      0))
